# massive crack-the-sky crontab — install with:  crontab deploy/crontab
#
# IMPORTANT: cron does NOT expand '~' or '$HOME' inside command strings, and
# does NOT expand variables in values. Before installing, replace the literal
# path /home/brad-lasater with your actual home directory, e.g.:
#     sed -i "s|/home/brad-lasater|$HOME|g" deploy/crontab
# (the username in the example path is a placeholder for the headless box user.)
#
# Priority order, because it explains the cadences below:
#   option_snapshots is the ONLY dataset here that cannot be backfilled. IV,
#   greeks, open interest and the underlying price exist solely at the moment
#   they are swept. Trades and bars can be re-pulled from S3 flat files years
#   later, so they run at whatever cadence is left over.
#
# Logs append to /data/massive/logs/cron.log (DATA_ROOT/logs). Structured
# per-run JSONL logs live under /data/massive/logs/<job>/dt=<date>/.
#
# flatfile_pull / reconcile / coverage_audit default --date to the previous
# trading day, so the Tue-Sat runs always target yesterday's session.
#
# underlying_bars runs T-1 only: same-day SPY minute aggs are not entitled on
# the Options Developer tier (403), so the 08:05 Tue-Sat run pulls yesterday.
TZ=America/New_York
MAILTO=""
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
REPO=/home/brad-lasater/crack-the-sky
DATA_ROOT=/data/massive
PY=/home/brad-lasater/crack-the-sky/venv/bin/python
LOG=/data/massive/logs/cron.log

# m h dom mon dow  command

# --- reference data -------------------------------------------------------
00 08 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh contracts_sync $PY -m ingest.jobs.contracts_sync >> $LOG 2>&1
30 16 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh contracts_sync $PY -m ingest.jobs.contracts_sync >> $LOG 2>&1
00 08 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh dividends_sync $PY -m ingest.jobs.dividends_sync >> $LOG 2>&1
# Saturday is never a trading day, so this line did nothing at all until the
# job learned to inject --force for the expired pass: run_job's gate exited 0
# before any work, and answered with a Healthchecks *ok* ping, so a job that
# had never once produced a partition monitored as healthy. The --force lives
# in contracts_sync.main(), not here, so a hand-run on a weekend behaves the
# same way. Do NOT add --expired to the Mon-Fri lines above: those must stay
# gated or they write a contracts partition on market holidays.
00 09 * * 6      cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh contracts_sync $PY -m ingest.jobs.contracts_sync --expired >> $LOG 2>&1
00 07 * * 0      cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh holidays_sync $PY -m ingest.jobs.holidays_sync >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- snapshots: 1/minute through the session (the irreplaceable dataset) ---
# A sweep of both chains takes ~13s wall (SPY and I:SPX run concurrently), so
# a 60s slot is comfortable; a run that would overlap is dropped, and
# scripts/cronjob.sh logs a job_skipped event so the drop is not silent.
# 09:05 pre-open captures the prior session's settled open interest.
05 09 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh snapshot_sweep $PY -m ingest.jobs.snapshot_sweep >> $LOG 2>&1
30-59 9 * * 1-5  cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh snapshot_sweep $PY -m ingest.jobs.snapshot_sweep >> $LOG 2>&1
* 10-15 * * 1-5  cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh snapshot_sweep $PY -m ingest.jobs.snapshot_sweep >> $LOG 2>&1
0-30 16 * * 1-5  cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh snapshot_sweep $PY -m ingest.jobs.snapshot_sweep >> $LOG 2>&1
35 16 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh snapshot_sweep $PY -m ingest.jobs.snapshot_sweep --eod >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- identity canary: after the 16:35 EOD snapshot has written today's
# snapshots + forwards. Own IV/Greeks from the as-of chain; pass/fail is
# ATM identities (reprice vs BSM(σ), European put-call Γ/vega/PCP).
# Vendor diffs are diagnostic when present; null vendor IV is not a FAIL.
# Bounded SPY CRR (--spy-atm-pct 0.05; --max-rows 400 caps CRR rows only).
# Exits non-zero on identity/data failure, which pings Healthchecks /fail
# and mails if MAILTO is set.
00 17 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh drift_check $PY -m pricing.drift_check >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- websocket minute bars (same-day tape) --------------------------------
25 09 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh ws_minute_bars $PY -m ingest.jobs.ws_minute_bars >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- same-day trades: every 5 min ------------------------------------------
# ~9,100 tickers once SPX is correctly banded; polled through a thread pool
# (TRADES_CONCURRENCY, default 8) bounded by the shared token bucket. Cold
# start is the slow run; later runs only fetch past each ticker's cursor.
# A median slot only has trades in ~850 of them, so tickers that keep coming
# back empty are polled every 2nd-4th slot (TRADES_BACKOFF_MAX, default 4;
# set 1 to disable). Skipping never advances a cursor, so it delays a trade's
# arrival and cannot drop one.
30-55/5 9 * * 1-5  cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh trades_watchlist $PY -m ingest.jobs.trades_watchlist >> $LOG 2>&1
*/5 10-15 * * 1-5  cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh trades_watchlist $PY -m ingest.jobs.trades_watchlist >> $LOG 2>&1
0-30/5 16 * * 1-5  cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh trades_watchlist $PY -m ingest.jobs.trades_watchlist >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- underlying ------------------------------------------------------------
05 08 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh underlying_bars $PY -m ingest.jobs.underlying_bars --prev-trading-day >> $LOG 2>&1
10 08 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh grouped_daily $PY -m ingest.jobs.grouped_daily >> $LOG 2>&1
# Underlying history chips away at the rolling entitlement window. The equity
# aggregate endpoints only serve ~2 years back (probed 2026-09-03: 2024-09-03
# is 200, 2024-06-03 is 403), so a session not fetched before it ages out is
# gone -- the only expiring dataset here other than option_snapshots.
#
# Bounded and paced deliberately. These endpoints throttle far harder than
# /v3/trades (grouped_daily returns the whole US market per call), and the API
# budget belongs to option_snapshots first, so this takes a small bite nightly
# at 02:30 -- far outside the capture window -- and finishes over a couple of
# weeks. Once the window is full it costs nothing: existing partitions are
# skipped without a request.
#
# No dates on the command line on purpose. The window is derived from
# coverage_audit's entitlement constant, so the start moves forward with the
# boundary. A pinned start would drift past it within a fortnight and spend
# the nightly budget re-requesting sessions that had already expired.
#
# Monitored twice over, because the two failures look nothing alike: its own
# Healthchecks entry catches the job dying or erroring, and coverage_audit's
# *_window checks catch the window developing a hole regardless of why. The
# first alone would miss a job that runs and quietly achieves nothing; the
# second alone would not notice until a gap drifted within 30 days of expiry.
30 02 * * *      cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh backfill_underlying $PY scripts/backfill_underlying.py --max-sessions 40 --sleep 6 >> $LOG 2>&1

# Treasury curve: the discount rate every IV inversion uses. 2 requests.
# History is already walked back to 1962; `--full` resumes if that changes.
20 08 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh rates_sync $PY -m ingest.jobs.rates_sync >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- T+1 flat files: the authoritative record ------------------------------
05 11 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh flatfile_pull $PY -m ingest.jobs.flatfile_pull >> $LOG 2>&1
30 11 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh reconcile $PY -m ingest.jobs.reconcile >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- broker executions (IBKR Flex; no gateway, read-only) ------------------
# 18:30 ET, after the trade date is final. A day with no fills is not an error.
30 18 * * 1-5    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh ibkr_executions $PY -m ingest.jobs.ibkr_executions >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- derived: ATM implied-vol term structure -------------------------------
# Reduces T-1 option_day_bars to one row per (root, expiry): parity forward,
# ATM strike, both leg IVs. Must run after flatfile_pull (11:05) has landed
# the day bars it reads. Being derived, a missed day is rebuilt with
# scripts/build_term_structure.py rather than lost -- but it is scheduled and
# monitored anyway, because a reduction that quietly stops producing is
# exactly as invisible as a capture that stops.
00 12 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh term_structure $PY -m pricing.term_structure >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- did we actually capture everything? -----------------------------------
# Runs after flatfile_pull (11:05) and reconcile (11:30) have settled T-1.
# Exits non-zero on any gap, which pings Healthchecks /fail.
30 12 * * 2-6    cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh coverage_audit $PY -m ingest.jobs.coverage_audit >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- is the whole archive still complete? ----------------------------------
# coverage_audit only ever looks at T-1, which is how 19 trading days in
# Feb-Apr 2023 sat missing for years while every daily run passed. This walks
# the entire history instead, and tells a holiday from a hole by asking the
# vendor whether a flat file exists for the date.
# Weekly, not daily: dates whose data is on disk need no probe, so a healthy
# run makes no network calls and takes ~4s. Saturday, after that morning's
# flatfile_pull has settled Friday.
00 13 * * 6      cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh history_audit $PY -m ingest.jobs.history_audit >> $LOG 2>&1

# --- retention: prune raw payloads reconstructible from flat files ---------
15 03 1 * *      cd $REPO && bash scripts/cronjob.sh prune bash scripts/prune_raw.sh --apply >> $LOG 2>&1

# NOTE: eod_dayaggs_rest is deliberately NOT scheduled. It made 42,162
# sequential REST calls (36 min) to produce ~16,700 rows that day_aggs_v1
# delivers in one 4 MB file the next morning. Kept as a manual tool:
#   venv/bin/python -m ingest.jobs.eod_dayaggs_rest --date YYYY-MM-DD --watchlist
